\", value is a JSON string containing the Python source code. Need to escape newlines and quotes properly.\n\nLet's craft the code:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    threshold = case['threshold']\n    calls = case['calls']\n    accepted = []\n    diagnostics = []\n    for c in calls:\n        call_id = c['call_id']\n        gain = c['loss_without_call'] - c['loss_with_call']\n        tokens = c['prediction_tokens']\n        if tokens <= 0:\n            tokens = 1\n        norm_gain = gain / tokens\n        is_accepted = c['parseable'] and norm_gain >= threshold\n        diagnostics.append({\n            'accepted': is_accepted,\n            'call_id': call_id,\n            'normalized_gain': round(norm_gain, 6)  # rounding to 6 decimals\n        })\n        if is_accepted:\n            accepted.append(call_id)\n    return {\n        'accepted_call_ids': accepted,\n        'diagnostics': diagnostics\n    }\n\nBut note: rounding might cause floating point issues. The output should be a JSON number. Use round(..., 6) as float. That's fine.\n\nWe must ensure we don't mutate case. We only read.\n\nNow, produce the JSON response. The entire response must be a JSON object with key \"implementation\". The value is the Python source as a JSON string. So we need to serialize the Python source into a JSON string, with proper escaping. I'll write the code as a multi-line string, then escape it. I'll use json.dumps on the Python source string.\n\nI'll produce output:\n\n{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    threshold = case['threshold']\\n    calls = case['calls']\\n    accepted = []\\n    diagnostics = []\\n    for c in calls:\\n        call_id = c['call_id']\\n        gain = c['loss_without_call'] - c['loss_with_call']\\n        tokens = c['prediction_tokens']\\n        if tokens <= 0:\\n            tokens = 1\\n        norm_gain = gain / tokens\\n        is_accepted = c['parseable'] and norm_gain >= threshold\\n        diagnostics.append({\\n            'accepted': is_accepted